// @check
// Soundness corpus index (ROADMAP A12).
//
// This directory pins down VL's type-safety contract: every well-typed program
// is type-safe at runtime (see docs/soundness.md). Each file is a small, focused
// "must-error" or "must-not-error" program so a future deep change to inference,
// narrowing, or codegen cannot silently regress soundness — a regression flips a
// must-not-error case into an error (or vice versa) and breaks `deno task test`.
//
// The runner is STRICT BY DEFAULT (see tests/cases_test.ts): a test fails on any error
// or warning it did not declare. So the two halves of the contract are:
//   // @check / @run with NO @error  — the "must-not-error" half: the file is
//             asserting it compiles with zero diagnostics (@check = not run,
//             @run = also runs and checks `@log` output end to end). No @ok
//             directive is needed — clean compile is the default.
//   // @error / @error-at            — the "must-error" half: an unsound use is
//             rejected with a diagnostic containing the given text.
//
// NAMING. A case's name says what it PINS, and the runner's verdict is the only
// thing that says whether it passes. Those are different axes, and conflating
// them has cost real time: six `xfail-*` files here were ordinary passing @error
// pins wearing a name that read "known failure", which is why ROADMAP A13 read
// OPEN long after its defect was fixed. So:
//   `*-reject.vl`  — a must-error pin. The @error is the DESIRED verdict: an
//             unsound (or otherwise illegal) program that the checker correctly
//             refuses. `arith-hole-operand-reject.vl`,
//             `generic-fn-param-mismatch-reject.vl`.
//   `*-sound.vl` / plain — a must-not-error pin, per the two halves above.
//   `xfail-*.vl`   — the compiler is WRONG here, and the file pins the wrong
//             behavior so that fixing it is loud instead of silent.
//
// `xfail-` alone is ambiguous, because a checker can be wrong in two opposite
// directions. The prefix therefore always carries which one:
//   `xfail-unsound-*.vl`      — ACCEPTS TOO MUCH. A program that should be
//             refused compiles clean (no @error, a `TODO(soundness):` note). The
//             gap is a hole in the type-safety contract. When it closes, the file
//             becomes an ordinary `*-reject.vl` @error pin — `array-element-
//             recursion.vl` and `arith-hole-operand-reject.vl` both made that trip.
//             There are currently NONE: the live unsound corner (container element
//             variance, `Cat[]` into `Animal[]`) is not pinned here at all — see
//             docs/internals/open-rulings.md.
//   `xfail-false-reject-*.vl` — REJECTS TOO MUCH. A SOUND program is refused, so
//             the file carries an @error pinning the UNDESIRED diagnostic. This is
//             a completeness gap, never a soundness one; closing it LOOSENS the
//             checker, which makes it reject-parity work. When it closes, drop the
//             prefix and flip the file to @run + @log.
//   `xfail-miscompile-*.vl`   — ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. A well-typed program
//             the checker is RIGHT to accept, which the emitter then lowers wrong:
//             `vl check --codegen` exits 0 and the module does not instantiate (or
//             the compiler crashes emitting it). Neither of the two above fits —
//             the program is not illegal, so it is not "accepts too much", and it
//             is not refused, so it is not "rejects too much". The failure is in
//             codegen, not in the type-safety contract.
//             SEVEN more were found and FIXED during the band-1
//             destringify programme (six check-clean invalid-wasm holes plus one
//             out-of-bounds compiler crash) and every one was FIXED in the same
//             change that found it, so each is pinned as an ordinary `@run` or
//             `@emit-error` case under tests/cases/generics/ instead. The prefix
//             exists for the one that cannot be fixed immediately — a defect of
//             this shape has no other home, which is why `docs/internals/
//             destringify-types-program.md` had to carry B9 in prose for four
//             slices before it was closed.
//             The channels that SEE this kind are `vl build` / `vl run` (they
//             instantiate) and, since the CMD_VALIDATE gate, `vl check --codegen`
//             (it hands its bytes to the engine and reports `invalid-module`);
//             plain `vl check` never emits, so it does not. Every file carrying
//             this directive is asserted to be flagged by that gate — and every
//             file the gate flags to carry the directive — by a set-equality
//             tripwire in tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts, so a member that gets
//             FIXED reddens the suite until its directive is deleted. The directive
//             is `@no-instantiate` — added WITH this kind, because the harness had
//             nothing that could pin it: `@trap` asserts a `VLRuntimeError` from a
//             module that DID load and explicitly rejects the CompileError a
//             miscompile produces. A bare `@no-instantiate` accepts any
//             instantiation failure; add TEXT to match the engine's message.
//             The kind's first member — `xfail-miscompile-permuted-struct-fields.vl`,
//             two field-permuted object types that were one type to the checker (the
//             2026-08 ruling) and two rows to the emitter — is CLOSED: the ruling was
//             carried into the emitter, and the file made this kind's documented trip,
//             flipping to `@run` as `tests/cases/types/permuted-named-alias-shares-row.vl`.
//             THERE IS ONE `@no-instantiate` MEMBER HERE NOW (see "THE LIVE MEMBERS"
//             below); every count in the history that follows is the count at the time
//             that line was written. Seven closed in one pass (see below), an eighth right
//             after it, then the NUMERIC
//             LITERAL-UNION pair — this kind's
//             `numeric-litunion-empty-list-seed` and the `xfail-false-reject-` file beside it
//             — closed together, and they are worth a sentence because BOTH headers had
//             mis-diagnosed themselves as blocked on a missing REPRESENTATION (one calling it
//             "a feature decision about that family", the other attributing it to ROADMAP
//             A16). The representation was already decided and already named in the compiler:
//             a numeric literal union reps as its BASE SCALAR (`numLitUnionBaseTy`), because
//             its members ARE `i32`/`i64`/`f64` values and there is no atom for A16's tag
//             scheme to encode. What was missing was the array-ELEMENT twin of that arm in
//             three ladders. Same sentence as the seven, one level less obvious. And before
//             all of those, the atom-widening family that briefly held four. That family's
//             shape is worth keeping: a literal union that reached the i32 ATOM rep was not
//             widened back to a string at several sinks, and the
//             four positions looked like one defect because they shared a symptom — an i32
//             where a reference was wanted. They were TWO defects. Three were VALUE sinks,
//             each fixed by asking for a widen that already existed. The fourth was a REP
//             DISAGREEMENT: a NARROWED literal-union PARAMETER is ALREADY declared `i32`, and
//             the predicates deciding whether to treat it as an atom asked the wrong
//             question. Fixed as tests/cases/literal-unions/narrowed-litunion-param-atom-rep.vl
//             by asking `tyLitUnionAliasIx`, which consults the checker's COPY-PROVENANCE
//             table as well as the alias registry. A STRUCTURAL test would have been wrong
//             and was tried first: it claims the atom rep for a bare inline `"ia" | "ib"`
//             too, which canon-softens to a string reference, so it swaps one miscompile for
//             another.
//             SEVEN CLOSED IN ONE PASS, and SIX OF THE SEVEN WERE ONE SENTENCE: a LADDER WITH
//             AN ARM ITS SIBLING LACKS. Written out, because the sentence is the reusable
//             part and the seven fixes are not:
//               · `atom-first-arraylit-ignores-annotation` — the adoption recorder stamped a
//                 destination array type on a literal whose element target is a NICHE-nullable
//                 scalar, and lacked the same arm for a plain `string` element over an
//                 all-atom literal. Now tests/cases/literal-unions/
//                 string-array-annotation-over-atom-elements.vl. Its header's claim that
//                 element ORDER was the trigger was WRONG and the graduated matrix says so.
//               · `nullable-elem-list-call-init` — `criClassify` had a returned-map-LOCAL rung
//                 and no returned-scalar-list-LOCAL one, so four element spellings that ride a
//                 scalar list's backing without being a `TyPrim` (`K[]`, `(K|null)[]`,
//                 `(boolean|null)[]`, `(string|null)[]`) took the i32 result default. Now
//                 tests/cases/lists/nullable-elem-list-call-init.vl, carrying all four.
//               · `newtype-string-inferred-return` — the checker's inferred-return recorder
//                 gates on `isClassifiableRetName`, which a BRANDED render (`NS`) fails, so no
//                 row was banked. It now banks the ERASED type. Now
//                 tests/cases/inference/newtype-string-inferred-return.vl.
//               · `nullable-ref-generic-arg` — `monoNulAnnName` (the guard that makes a
//                 nullable pin carry its `| null`) sat only on the arms behind `fnIx >= 0`;
//                 the identical program inside a function body always worked. The MODULE-SCOPE
//                 twin one line away had been widened for the same reason a release earlier.
//                 Now tests/cases/generics/nullable-arg-generic.vl.
//               · `toplevel-nested-array-inferred-empty` — `collectLocals`' empty-`[]`
//                 annotation synthesis is now one home with two callers, the second a
//                 module-scope pass over `globalStmts`. Now tests/cases/arrays/
//                 toplevel-nested-array-inferred-empty-push.vl.
//               · `lambda-captures-inferred-nonI32-elem` — BOTH of `criClassify`'s channels
//                 are blind at once for a local inside a MONOMORPHIZED instance's lambda: the
//                 locals table is unbuilt and the clone's leaves carry no recorded type. It
//                 now asks the DECLARATION (`letCellKind`). Its own header's diagnosis (the
//                 capture cell taking the i32 slot) was WRONG — the local was always right and
//                 the RESULT valtype was the i32 default, which disassembling the bad module
//                 says in one line. Now tests/cases/closures/lambda-captures-inferred-elem.vl.
//                 The same rung graduated two `@emit-error` f32-list fixtures that had
//                 SCRIPTED it as their prerequisite (tests/cases/arrays/inferred-f32-list-
//                 return*.vl) — worth knowing when grading a coverage zero.
//               · the seventh is not a ladder: `prim-named-type-alias`, a `type` declaration
//                 taking a PRIMITIVE's name (`type f64 = "a" | "b"`). The checker resolved that
//                 name through `primTyOfName` FIRST while the emitter's alias registry took it
//                 verbatim. It is now a DIAGNOSTIC at the declaration, which is what the xfail
//                 said the fix was; nothing in the tree declares one. Now
//                 tests/cases/types/prim-named-type-alias-rejected.vl.
//             THE EIGHTH CLOSED RIGHT AFTER, AND IT IS THE SAME SENTENCE A NINTH TIME:
//             `union-arm-constructed-map-field` — `emitObj` (the plain struct-literal path)
//             seeds a NULLABLE-map field's construct context under `scode == 29`; the
//             VARIANT-literal twin `emitVariantObj` had the `vcode == 19` (non-null map) arm
//             and no `vcode == 29` one, while BOTH read-back sites already paired `19 || 29`
//             off the same `uFieldMapShape`. So a `Map()` in a nullable-map ARM field reached
//             `emitMapNew` on the ambient `pendingMapSlot` (-1, the MONO STRING-keyed struct)
//             and built that row into a field declaring another. Now tests/cases/maps/
//             union-arm-constructed-map-field.vl. ITS HEADER'S THREE CONTROLS WERE READ ONE
//             CONJUNCT TOO NARROW — they concluded the i32 KEY was required. -1 is the mono
//             string-keyed shape, so a seed-less `Map()` is accidentally right for exactly
//             one field type and wrong for every other: `{[string]: string} | null`
//             miscompiles identically with no i32 anywhere, and a bare `null` in that field
//             failed LOUDER still for want of the same arm's `pendingNulRefHeap` seed. Five
//             shapes, one arm. (The header's closing claim — that the defect is in the
//             LOWERING and not in how `mI32Used` came to be set — was RIGHT, and is what
//             kept the search off the collect rungs.)
//             TWO HEADERS WERE WRONG ABOUT THEIR OWN DEFECT and both were written by careful
//             people. Re-derive a filed diagnosis before you build on it: for a miscompile the
//             re-derivation is `wasm-tools print` on the bad module, which costs seconds.
//             TWO MORE CLOSED, AND ONE FILED GROUPING WAS WRONG WHILE ANOTHER FILED
//             INTERLOCK WAS RIGHT. Both are worth the paragraph because the corrections run
//             in opposite directions.
//               · `xfail-miscompile-narrowed-litunion-fn-value-arg.vl` — a narrowed litunion
//                 parameter reached through a FUNCTION VALUE — is now
//                 `tests/cases/literal-unions/narrowed-litunion-fn-value-arg.vl`. Its filed
//                 diagnosis named THREE `$fnsig` producers that had to move "byte-identical
//                 or nothing" (`cloParamTok`, `annSigKey`, `sigKeyOfTy`). Re-derived, that
//                 was two errors: `cloParamTok` was ALREADY right (it reads the rep layer),
//                 and `annSigKey` CANNOT be fixed — its whole input is a rendered spelling,
//                 and a narrowed litunion COPY renders exactly like a genuinely INLINE one
//                 while repping differently, so the deciding fact is not in the string. One
//                 producer moved (`sigKeyOfTy`, onto the copy-provenance table
//                 `tyLitUnionAliasIx`) and the CONSUMER resolves the residual tie: a call
//                 whose name-derived key names no interned `$fnsig` takes the arena key when
//                 that one does. "Byte-identical or nothing" is the wrong frame when one
//                 producer is provably blind — the answer there is to rank the producers, not
//                 to align them.
//                 `tests/cases/std/xfail-miscompile-array-litunion-element.vl` was grouped
//                 with it as "the same question". It is NOT: closing the above changed
//                 nothing there. Its minimal witness is now pinned here as
//                 `xfail-miscompile-inline-litunion-element-read.vl` — four lines, no std, no
//                 generic, no closure — and it is a `ctxKeepsLitUnion` FAMILY DECISION.
//               · `xfail-miscompile-permuted-object-closure-arms.vl` — a union whose two
//                 closure arms are ONE TYPE — is now
//                 `tests/cases/closures/permuted-object-closure-arms.vl`. Its header called
//                 the interlock with #1864 correctly (neither half works alone) and named the
//                 wrong second half: the RENDER SORT it proposed was built and MEASURED, and
//                 sorting `tyToEmitNameAt` needs `tyToNominalNameAt` with it and STILL forks,
//                 because a fourth producer — `canonEmitNameAt`, which canons the SOURCE
//                 ANNOTATION TEXT — keys declaration order (`types/nullable-union-alias.vl`
//                 and `literal-unions/inline-atom-shape-field.vl` both go red). What landed
//                 instead is two lines, neither a render: `emitIs`' union-box arm asks the
//                 STORAGE question as well as the registry one (`identSlotIsUnionBox`), and
//                 `tySameAt` compares object fields by SET rather than by POSITION. When a
//                 header says "these must move together", believe the interlock and re-derive
//                 the halves.
//             THE TWO THAT REMAIN, and why each is left:
//               · `xfail-miscompile-inline-litunion-element-read.vl` — an element read out of
//                 an INLINE literal-union array. `ctxKeepsLitUnion` PRESERVES a litunion's
//                 members at `RC_ELEM` and SOFTENS them at `RC_ROOT`/`RC_FN_PARAM`, so one
//                 spelling has two reps and they meet at every element read. The DECLARED
//                 alias spelling of the same program is green, which is what says this is
//                 about the inline spelling and not about the family. Closing it means
//                 CHOOSING one position-independent rule; that header names the two candidate
//                 position rules already built and refuted. It has a SILENT sibling
//                 (`const v: "a" | "b" = tags[0]` prints the raw atom id) with no fixture kind.
//               · `xfail-miscompile-totality-gate-blind-to-nested-null.vl` — the odd one out:
//                 a LOUD reject, on the checker side, and it carries an ORDERING CONSTRAINT in
//                 its header that must be read before touching it.
//             THOSE TWO ARE NOW CLOSED AS WELL, and the paragraph above is kept for its
//             account rather than its inventory. `inline-litunion-element-read` and the
//             GENERIC rung beside it graduated in #1910 (the boundary WIDEN, gated on a
//             predicate that answers from the receiver's PARAM ANNOTATION — a monomorphized
//             instance SHARES its leaf expression nodes with the generic body, so those nodes
//             record the GENERIC's types and its annotations are the only concrete fact it
//             carries); the `std/` file they left behind — `sorted` over an inline-litunion
//             array — graduated with the last two boundaries of the same family, and
//             `generic-litunion-element-bind-and-store.vl` here is the `@run` pin that
//             replaced it. THE `ctxKeepsLitUnion` FAMILY DECISION BOTH HEADERS SAID WAS
//             REQUIRED WAS NOT REQUIRED: the two reps were reconciled at the boundaries
//             instead, and the position rule is untouched.
//             THE LIVE MEMBERS: none from this family. The whole family graduated together, and
//             the shape of that is the record worth keeping. What refilled this directory came
//             from OUTSIDE it, in the same commit — see the nullable-callback row at the end.
//               * `xfail-miscompile-array-litunion-needle.vl` — a bare member LITERAL handed
//                 to `indexOf`/`lastIndexOf`/`includes`/`count` over a litunion array — is now
//                 `tests/cases/std/array-litunion-needle.vl`. The root was one rung below the
//                 filing: the pin a monomorphized instance takes BECOMES its parameter's
//                 annotation, and for a literal-union array it named the argument's REP
//                 (`nameIsI32Array` accepts `K[]`) instead of its TYPE, so `T` bound to plain
//                 `i32`. The same arm closed a LOUD sibling the filing never mentioned: at
//                 FUNCTION scope the identical call was `monomorphize: expected an array
//                 argument for `self``, which took the documented remedy down with it.
//               * `xfail-miscompile-fntype-litunion-result-call.vl` — the tripwire's specimen
//                 source — is folded into
//                 `tests/cases/literal-unions/fntype-litunion-result-controls.vl`, all eight
//                 cells running. `ctxKeepsLitUnion` is now false at `RC_FN_RES`: the three
//                 surviving preserve positions are CONTAINERS, and a function RESULT is the
//                 same scalar position a DECLARATION's return annotation occupies. The
//                 `compiler/typecheck.vl` comment that once said the asymmetry was "not
//                 reachable as a miscompile", and then said it was, now says it is closed.
//               * `xfail-miscompile-mapindexed-litunion-callback-binding.vl` is now
//                 `tests/cases/std/array-mapindexed-litunion-callback-delivery.vl`, the
//                 DELIVERY × SPELLING cross product its predecessor's grid took the diagonal
//                 of. The missing fact was a TWIN: `cloCallParamLitUnion` had told the emitter
//                 a value call's PARAMETER is an atom since function values learned to take a
//                 `K`, and nothing said it about the RESULT.
//
//             THE CLASS WAS BRIEFLY EMPTY, and `tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts`'s corpus
//             tripwire needs a live member or it reads nothing. Its specimen left the family
//             entirely: a `u8[]` handed to a `std:array` generic, pre-existing and recorded
//             unpinned in `std/array.vl` since #1927, is now
//             `tests/cases/std/xfail-miscompile-u8-array-generic-needle.vl`. The tripwire's
//             "read nothing" guard moved onto the SCAN rather than the class — requiring a
//             member conflates a scan that walked no files with a class that is genuinely
//             empty, which is the outcome every fix here works toward.
//
//             AND `soundness/` REFILLED IN THE SAME COMMIT, from a shape with nothing to do
//             with literal unions: `xfail-miscompile-narrowed-nullable-fn-arg.vl` — a nullable
//             function value narrowed non-null with `is` and then PASSED AS AN ARGUMENT kept
//             its `(ref null …)` valtype across the call while the parameter declared the
//             non-null one. Found by the STD REVIEW of this commit, against this commit's own
//             clearance, on an optional COMPARATOR — the most ordinary shape there is. It is
//             element-type-independent (an `i32` callback failed identically), std-independent
//             (a non-generic function taking a function parameter reproduced it), and calling
//             the narrowed value in place ran. That is the pattern this directory exists to
//             record: the review that found it was licensed by the ledger rule the same commit
//             was invoking, and the counter-example was not in the family the commit was about.
//
//             IT IS NOW CLOSED, and it graduated to `narrowed-nullable-fn-arg.vl` here plus
//             `std/narrowed-nullable-comparator-delivery.vl` for the four caller-facing
//             spellings. The root was the call boundary's non-null recover ladder: it held the
//             coercion for `struct` and for the seven list/map wrapper kinds and had never
//             learned the two remaining bare-`(ref $heap)` reps, `closure` and `variant`. The
//             VARIANT half was found only by GRIDDING the rep axis — `Circle | null` narrowed
//             into a `Circle` parameter is the same defect and no filed witness named it —
//             which is `narrowed-nullable-variant-arg.vl`.
//
//             AND THE HYPOTHESIS THAT THESE WERE ONE RUNG IS REFUTED. They share a root — a
//             producer naming one literal-union type two ways across a boundary — and they
//             took FOUR separate changes, each with its own witness, which is `Root E`'s
//             standing warning read the other way: a shared root does not imply one fix. What
//             the grouping bought was the CROSS PRODUCT (210 cells, delivery × spelling ×
//             position, 173 → 210 running), and that is what found the two the filings had
//             not: the loud function-scope half of the `needle` row, and D13's map-value
//             destination.
//               (`xfail-miscompile-nulstr-list-coalesce.vl` stood beside it and GRADUATED —
//               `??` over a LIST INDEX whose element is `string | null` typed its if-expression
//               at the non-null string it promises and RE-READ the nullable backing in the else
//               arm. The re-read is gone: the `string | null` `??` now takes the
//               `br_on_non_null` block every other nullable-REF niche already used, so the
//               place is evaluated ONCE and the narrow IS the branch — which closed a second
//               evaluation of the index expression at the same time. It is now
//               `tests/cases/lists/nullable-elem-list-coalesce.vl`, `@run` + `@log`.)
//               (`tests/cases/std/xfail-miscompile-mapindexed-litunion-result.vl` GRADUATED
//               too, and with it the `std/` half of the two-directory scan: a monomorphized
//               instance was binding its body's annotated locals from the pin NAME and its
//               RETURN annotation from the ARGUMENT's arena row, and the two disagree at a
//               FUNCTION-TYPE parameter, whose pin renders the type parameter's SOFTENED
//               spelling — so one instance spelled `U[]` as both an atom-backed and a
//               string-backed list. It is now
//               `tests/cases/std/array-mapindexed-litunion-result.vl`. `std/` did not stay
//               empty for long — the needle row above landed in it in the same commit — but
//               the scan would keep both directories either way: an empty directory costs
//               one invocation, a dropped one is how the class moved out from under the gate
//               once already.)
//               (The totality-gate row that stood here GRADUATED in #1914 and is now
//               `totality-gate-nested-null.vl`; a std review caught this inventory still
//               listing it as live, which is the one-directional staleness this file's own
//               header warns about.)
//               THE RELATIONAL-COMPARE ROW HAS GRADUATED TOO, and is now
//               `litunion-alias-relational-compare.vl` — still here, still `@run` + `@log`,
//               now pinning the RIGHT answers. It was the directory's only WRONG-VALUE pin:
//               `<` `>` `<=` `>=` over a NAMED literal-union alias compared interned IDS, so
//               the ordering was by first appearance in the emitted code. `emitBinExprNode`
//               reached its numeric arm because BOTH operands were already i32 (the alias
//               reps as the atom) and nothing asked whether they were atoms; `exprIsStrOrder`
//               now asks `strOrderOperandIsText` and `emitStrOrder` pushes each operand
//               through `emitStrValue`, so the atom widens to its member string first. `==`
//               was unaffected throughout (equal ids iff equal texts) and is untouched —
//               byte-identical A/B — as are NUMERIC literal unions, which are not litunions
//               to `exprIsLitAtom` at all. The grid lives at
//               `tests/cases/literal-unions/litunion-order-is-member-text.vl` and
//               `litunion-order-cross-module-alias.vl`, and `std/array.vl`'s ORDERING
//               carve-out was DELETED rather than softened. Both of the survivors it
//               renumbered have since been deleted the same way — they were the two
//               `mapIndexed` rows, closed when the monomorphizer stopped spelling one type
//               parameter two ways inside one instance
//               (`tests/cases/std/array-mapindexed-litunion-result.vl` is the graduated
//               grid). The two that renumbering left — the bare-literal `needle` ARGUMENT and
//               `reduce`'s accumulator — have since been deleted the same way, with the rest
//               of the literal-union BOUNDARY class, so that module now carries NONE. Its
//               over-promise LEDGER still counts them; the carve-outs themselves are gone.
//             AND TWO WRONG-VALUE SHAPES STILL HAVE NO KIND HERE. The relational-compare pin
//             narrowed that gap while it was open rather than closing it, and its graduation
//             does not reopen it: a wrong value that IS PRINTED can be pinned with `@run` +
//             `@log` — in either direction — and the two below cannot.
//             #1867 (a permuted OBJECT
//             union arm: `u is PB` FALSE for a PB, from `structFieldCodesEq` being POSITIONAL)
//             and the silent sibling above both COMPILE, VALIDATE, run and exit 0 with a wrong
//             answer. `@no-instantiate` cannot pin a valid module and `@trap` explicitly
//             rejects one; `@run` + `@log` cannot assert that output is ABSENT. Two instances
//             is enough to say the gap is in the harness, not only in the compiler.
// Renaming across these is not cosmetic — the name is what a reader (and the
// ROADMAP) uses to decide whether an item is still open.
//
// This file is itself a trivial must-not-error case so the directory is never
// empty. `_corpus` is intentionally unread (leading `_`) -> a faded hint, not a
// warning (asserted below to keep the strict harness green).
// @hint Intentionally-unused variable `_corpus`
let _corpus = 0
